Alleles on chromosomes can be cut by a certain restriction endonuclease to form relatively independent DNA fragments. These alleles can be separated by gel electrophoresis after enzyme digestion, and different band patterns can be displayed after hybridization with a probe. A couple has two children, one of whom, number 4, has a genetic disease. Figure B shows the results of genetic testing for individuals 1 to 4 in Figure A. Which of the following inferences is incorrect? ( )  
A. The DNA fragment with a length of 7.0 units is the pathogenic gene and is located on an autosome.
B. The probability that individual 3 has the same genotype as the father is \frac{2} {3} 
C. If individual 3 marries a carrier of the disease gene, the probability of having a child with the disease is \frac{1} {4} 
D. Alleles are not only reflected in different base sequences, but their lengths can also vary.

